  - fixed drift in `docs/wiki/testing.md`, which claimed "Benchmarks use JMH and feed `EvaluationEvidence`"; they do not on any runnable path, which is the same overstatement removed from the README
  - follow-up committed directly to `main` with explicit user authorisation: the first pass cut the genetic-programming rationale too hard and left `Background` at position 13 of 20, behind the configuration tables, with the word "experiment" gone from the opening entirely. Restored as `## Why this shape` positioned between the fitness audit and the agent-plus-CI comparison, carrying the 1990s GP loop, the argument for a population (asking twice gives two answers with no shared axis, so ideas in rejected attempts do not survive), and the reason recombination is sequenced behind single-candidate evaluation rather than beside it (recombining unreliable evidence produces unreliable children). README 3383 -> 3512 words, 21.9% below the 4498 baseline
  - implemented step 1 of the fitness taxonomy design: `check-glossary` enforcing seven load-bearing terms, glossary entries separating candidate fitness from architecture fitness and benchmark from corpus, and `CON-002` recording the `subject`/`process` plus `invariant`/`objective` naming scheme and the integrity/safety/correctness/shape severity classes
  - deliberately did not rename `hard_gate_*` identifiers: the rename changes MCP output ordering asserted by `EvolveMcpToolTest` and invalidates identifiers already persisted in any target repository that has run `saaa-evolve`, both in its `.saaa/experiments.sqlite` ledger and in its `experiments/ledger/*.toon` envelopes, so it needs its own change with a migration story
  - task review caught two defects in the CON-002 entry: a stray code fence copied from the task brief's own formatting, and a claim that Gradle makes any layer violation a compile error. The second was corrected after human ruling: the module direction is Gradle-enforced, but package-level provider confinement is enforced by `check-architecture-boundaries` under `project lint`, because merging the adapter modules put langchain4j, sqlite and flyway on one compile classpath
  - fixed all findings from the whole-branch code review of this branch: corrected the Benchmark glossary entry to describe the intended `cost_latency_budget` wiring rather than claiming `BenchmarkEvidence` is produced today, appended a "target model, not yet in force" disclaimer to Invariant/Objective/Severity Class, corrected the persisted-identifier claim in CON-002 and in this handoff's own `completed` history to say "any target repository that has run `saaa-evolve`" rather than implying this repository persists experiment state, corrected the design note's `process.invariant.layer_boundaries` section to match CON-002's already-corrected `check-architecture-boundaries` wording instead of the superseded "Gradle makes any layer violation a compile error" claim, recorded the `check-glossary` fitness-function delta and a no-`CHG`-spec rationale below, added `project check-glossary` to the `AGENTS.md` canonical command table and folded the glossary check into the `project check` row's enumeration, reordered `## Objective` to sit after `## Northbound Interface` restoring alphabetical order (and removed the now-stale risk entry describing that break), and added the missing trailing newline to CON-002

in_progress: []

next:
  - obtain independent PR review for CHG-012; its policy check proves discoverability, while Q-010 retains the empirical routing-economics gate
  - obtain a completed independent reviewer verdict for CHG-011 PR #29 before merge; the OpenCode CLI event stream did not expose the completed Flex sessions' final text
  - design a measured `model-routing-change` slice only after proposer usage and provider budget evidence are available
  - decide how to transport `MutationContract` and typed required-evidence through the live loop before implementing contract-aware scoring (CHG-002 T4b, RISK-002)
  - decide what to do about `task_success`, `reliability`, `cost_latency_budget` and `behavioral_safety` all being constant for eligible candidates, which leaves `parsimony` as the only discriminating objective and makes the 0.80 threshold unreachable from below
  - make a repeat fixture run fail before the decision rather than after: the ledger rejects a second differing outcome for the same candidate id, so the run journals its verdict and then exits non-zero with a stack trace
  - run a fixed, reviewed mutation corpus with a real proposer/embedding provider across NONE, VECTOR, GRAPH and HYBRID; no GraphRAG benefit has been measured yet
  - use the first measured ablation to decide whether Neo4j relationship traversal contributes enough distinct evidence to retain ADR-0004's experiment
  - close CHG-004 with `T11/T12`: README/wiki/profile/handoff updates for the openai-compatible profile, MCP surface, classical guards, Layer-3 constraints and Q-001 answer
  - CHG-003 follow-ups remain: `T9` (reporters surface why a check failed) and `T12` (pre-flight validates `HEAD` rather than the working tree); note the CHG-004 `T9` is a separate concern (compiler failure as failed check) sharing only the id
  - fold RISK-003 into the population slice rather than fixing it separately, because that slice needs several live candidate worktrees at once and has to choose a candidate naming scheme anyway
  - implement `T3d` TOON mutation contract envelope parser feeding the canonicalizer
  - migrate `MutationEvaluationLoop` from the transitional `Mutation`/`MutationValidator`/`FitnessScorer` stack onto `MutationContract` and `PhenotypeFitnessScorer`; both stacks exist today, only the old one is wired
  - implement remaining CHG-002 policy tasks T2, T5 and T6 before any live provider run that relies on those guarantees
  - the population slice: evaluate several candidates per generation and rank them, calling `MutationEvaluationLoop.evaluate` N times

decisions:
  - id: bounded_model_session_efficiency
    summary: provider sessions are disposable execution state reused only within one objective, role and permission scope; explicit route/budget/usage evidence and fresh independent-review sessions protect auditability without adding automatic routing
  - id: bounded_evolutionary_memory_archive
    summary: Git TOON envelopes are durable rebuild input, experiment SQLite is the current efficient ledger, the wiki is a non-authoritative projection and Neo4j is a policy-bounded working graph
  - id: subject_process_evolution_context
    summary: one partitioned graph relates both subject and SAAA process revisions to each deterministic evaluation and retrieval configuration
  - id: jgit_first_native_fallback
    summary: comparable read-only Git operations use pinned JGit with no manual setup; native Git fallback is visible and remains for established linked candidate-worktree semantics
  - id: saaa_command_namespace
    summary: installed binary is saaa and automation CLI/MCP command tokens carry a saaa prefix; `saaa sa` is the deliberate interactive-session exception
  - id: interactive_harness_session_control_plane
    summary: `saaa sa` records explicit target and registered profile choices, dispatches through the existing governed loop, and leaves automatic routing and agent approval authority out of the session
  - id: local_hybrid_graphrag_experiment
    summary: ADR-0004 intentionally revisits the earlier deferral with optional local Neo4j and a separate derived retrieval SQLite lifecycle; repository artifacts remain canonical
  - id: retrieval_evidence_not_authority
    summary: retrieval treatment is explicit before LangChain4j invocation; evidence biases proposals while the unchanged deterministic loop decides survival
  - id: mutation_contract_retrieval_bridge
    summary: RetrievalQuery references optional MutationContract and uses transitional task text only until the live loop converges on MutationContract
  - id: langchain4j_adapter_boundary
    summary: LangChain4j stays in the `adapters/langchain4j` package of `modules/adapters`; `modules/domain` and `modules/deterministic` stay provider-free
  - id: deterministic_approval_boundary
    summary: model output may propose or repair but validation, scoring, promotion and rollback are deterministic
  - id: mutation_contract_ir_policy
    summary: TOON is the reviewable mutation-contract envelope; canonical S-expression is the internal mutation/operator IR; Git diffs are candidate realization evidence
  - id: closed_operator_enum
    summary: ten closed operator values; `conceptual-crossover` is deliberately absent and negatively tested
  - id: conceptual_crossover_boundary
    summary: conceptual crossover is a recombination policy producing a bounded child mutation contract; the policy validates its own child before emitting it
  - id: phenotype_fitness_policy
    summary: hard gates run before weighted objectives and are not tradeable; promotion threshold is `0.80` compared against the raw sum, with rounding applied to the reported score only
  - id: canonical_ir_safety
    summary: unquoted IR positions are checked against a safe atom/identifier shape so model-authored text cannot forge nodes such as an extra gate
  - id: gate_outcomes_win_in_audit_record
    summary: gate outcomes are written after measured scores in `FitnessResult.objectives` so evidence content cannot overwrite a recorded gate result
  - id: gates_live_with_the_scorer
    summary: every promotion invariant belongs in `PhenotypeFitnessScorer`, not in the bridge or the CLI, so promotion integrity does not depend on the caller assembling evidence correctly; `T10` follows the behaviour-case gate in this
  - id: realization_measured_in_files
    summary: the non-empty realization gate tests `filesChanged > 0` rather than `linesChanged`, so a mode-only change counts as a realization

blocking_questions: []

design_input:
  - question: what should behavioral_safety semantically measure, and from what evidence
    owner_position: deterministic scripts are absolute and belong at a very high binary threshold; LLM reasoning is valuable but non-absolute, taken above a confidence threshold from a quorum of cheap runs; possibly reward an upward trend; possibly be more permissive early when bootstrapping
    codex_position: a separate reviewer fixes the conflict of interest in RISK-001 but not the authority question in ARCH-001. If the deterministic reducer promotes partly because a reviewer says the candidate is safe, the reviewer has scored it in substance; deterministic arithmetic around a model verdict hides the judgement rather than removing it
    synthesis:
      - critical forbidden-action probes are a hard gate, all pass or discard
      - predeclared non-critical robustness probes contribute a deterministic pass fraction as the 0.10 behavioral_safety objective
      - reviewer models discover missing probes and escalate ambiguity to a human; probes enter the suite through the normal reviewed change process, so LLM reasoning generates deterministic tests rather than grading candidates
      - trend is a selection rule applied after absolute eligibility, not a weighted objective; SearchPosture's own contract says the posture never decides fitness, and adding trend to the sum would double-count objectives and make candidates from different parents incomparable
      - early permissiveness may apply to experiment budgets, non-safety objectives and promotion destination such as a quarantined ref, never to safety or authority gates, and any maturity policy must be fixed before candidate generation, versioned and non-retroactive
    correction: the claim that the weighted sum floors at 0.90 is true only of the currently wired path. budgetScore returns min(1.0, budget/value) and does vary; it reads 1.0 because the benchmark list is empty, so cost_latency_budget is starved rather than inert by design

risks:
  - CHG-012's textual contract check ensures the operating controls remain discoverable but cannot establish provider economics or quality; automatic routing remains blocked on Q-010's measured-usage, price-identity and ablation evidence
  - retaining a provider session can carry stale conclusions; the policy requires a reset for objective, role, privacy, transport or response-reserve changes and for independent review
  - a check script chosen as the mutation target grades its own rewrite: `EvolveRunner` requires only that `--workflow-file` is a regular non-symlink file inside the target folder, so `--workflow-file workflow-check.sh --behaviour-case workflow-check` realizes the proposal into the script the candidate is then measured by. Found during the README evidence pass; not fixed there
  - the weighted objectives cannot currently reject anything through the wired bridge: `task_success` restates its own hard gate, `reliability` only drops when a check already failed, `cost_latency_budget` has no evidence source because `:cli` does not depend on `:benchmarks` and `EvolveRunner` supplies a constant empty benchmark list, and `behavioral_safety` is the literal `1.0`. The sum floors at 0.90 against a 0.80 threshold, so eligibility is the whole decision and the weights are inert scaffolding
  - reliability now uses structured `TIMED_OUT` evidence; legacy envelopes with only diagnostic summaries retain their historical status values but cannot reconstruct timeout semantics unless they were recorded as `TIMED_OUT`
  - a Gradle run reporting `up-to-date` executes nothing, so it is not evidence; a recorded pass from a prior session was carried forward as green when the tests had not run, and clean-checkout CI then failed. Treat unit-test evidence as valid only from a run that actually executed, and prefer `--rerun-tasks` or a clean checkout before claiming a suite passes
  - unit tests that reach ambient environment state can pass locally and fail on clean runners; `graphrag-integration-test` and the Neo4j suite still depend on a local container and never run in CI, so that class of defect has less protection there than the unit suites do
  - RISK-004: revision-unsafe caches or uncapped historical outcome evidence could contaminate ablation treatments and create self-reinforcing search bias
  - RISK-005: current official Neo4j Community images retain vendor Java dependency findings even though the selected UBI image has no high/critical OS-package findings; keep Bolt loopback-only and upgrade/retest when the vendor ships fixes
  - `lineage-novelty-v1` is a plausible policy, not a proven one; its slot mix may discard useful historical niches, so envelopes remain durable and policy IDs/overrides must be included in ablation
  - policy selection currently scans the Git envelope archive in memory and graph memory replay writes at most 96 retained records one transaction at a time; this is bounded in the active graph but not yet measured at a large archive, so batch/index only after profiling shows pressure
  - cross-repository process facts and subject facts coexist in one graph but the first retrieval bundle remains scoped to the configured repository; explicit context links enable analysis now while cross-repository evidence fusion needs its own measured slice
  - Neo4j is justified only if bounded relationship traversal contributes distinct evidence; the ablation result must be allowed to reject it
  - PR fast CI now includes component and integration tests because CHG-004 uses those suites as load-bearing evidence; watch GitHub Actions duration and split further only when measured runtime or quota pressure justifies it
  - RISK-002: the scorer enforces its own fixed gates and never sees the contract, so a declared `required_evidence_present` gate can pass without the evidence it names; tracked as `T4b`
  - RISK-003: ordinary single evolve runs still derive candidate names from workflow and mutation ids; the ablation harness now supplies a stable experiment/task/mode/attempt namespace, while a general population naming policy remains deferred
  - Q-007: canonical IR preserves declared order for set-like fields, so contracts differing only in evidence order canonicalize differently
  - Q-009: Quarkus may become useful when SAAA has a durable HTTP/MCP runtime shell, but adopting it now would risk moving focus from mutation-loop behaviour to framework integration
  - MCP `targetFolder` accepts any local Git repository visible to the stdio server process and executes declared check scripts from that repository; acceptable for the local developer tool, but unsafe as-is for a remote or multi-user service
  - MCP stdio lifecycle is only startable in this slice (`saaa mcp` starts a stdio server and waits under the supervising client/process); there is no transport-level ATDD yet proving client-disconnect shutdown behavior, so do not claim more than "startable MCP stdio entrypoint" until a follow-up test drives the real process lifecycle
  - behaviour-check environment scrubbing removes provider credentials but is not a full sandbox; candidate code can still use allowed tools and local filesystem/network capabilities available to the check process
  - the new `MutationContract` / `MutationContractValidator` / `PhenotypeFitnessScorer` stack is parallel to the wired `Mutation` / `MutationValidator` / `FitnessScorer` ports; the design records `Mutation.patch` as transitional but no migration step is scheduled yet
  - benchmark presence is not yet a hard gate because "required benchmark evidence" is contract-dependent and the scorer has no contract input
  - the fixture proposer is deterministic by design, so it cannot demonstrate that a real model produces useful mutations; it only proves the pipe
  - `behavioral_safety` has no independent evidence source in this slice and is fixed at 1.0, leaving one of five objectives inert
  - the non-empty realization gate rejects a no-op candidate but says nothing about whether the change is meaningful; a one-character whitespace edit still passes it, so parsimony's pull toward the smallest diff is bounded rather than removed
  - `journal.md` is written into the user's target folder, so the command writes outside its own repository
  - the `.saaa` pathspec exclusion in `GitRealizationInspector` is coupled by convention to `GitCandidateWorkspace`, which writes bookkeeping under `.saaa/candidates/`; a future adapter writing bookkeeping elsewhere would need the exclusion updated
  - model/provider cost is reported only when supplied; current LangChain4j evidence captures input/output tokens but has no configured price schedule, so ablation cost falls back to token count
  - reporters print only check name and status, so a behaviour case that fails for its own reasons shows no diagnostics; tracked as `CHG-003` task `T9`
  - the CLI pre-flight inspects the working tree while checks run against `HEAD`, so an uncommitted or mode-mismatched script passes pre-flight and then fails to start inside the candidate; tracked as `CHG-003` task `T12`
  - check scripts run in a worktree created from `HEAD`, so a new check script must be committed before it can gate a run
  - the behaviour case convention is POSIX-shaped, so `evolve` is effectively Linux and macOS only; on a case-insensitive filesystem a single case named `Foo` is satisfied by `foo.sh`

